WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
Draw a soft brush across the hairy skin of a human forearm.

2
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.020
Go slowly, then faster.

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.880
One class of slow nerve fibre changes how hard it fires.

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.280
Ask people to rate the same strokes, and they call the middle speeds the most

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.320
pleasant. Is that why a slow stroke can feel nice?

6
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:25.053
The fibre is called a C-tactile afferent, or CT afferent.

7
00:00:25.553 --> 00:00:29.913
It is a touch fibre with an unmyelinated axon, so it has no insulating wrap.

8
00:00:30.553 --> 00:00:34.333
The name is often used for what mouse papers call a C-LTMR.

9
00:00:34.693 --> 00:00:37.953
Whether the two names mean the same cells is a question we return to.

10
00:00:38.553 --> 00:00:41.953
To listen to one fibre, researchers use microneurography.

11
00:00:42.413 --> 00:00:44.973
A fine electrode goes through the skin into a nerve.

12
00:00:45.373 --> 00:00:47.933
It records single axons in an awake person.

13
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:48.933
For more information, please visit www .cst .com

14
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:54.203
Löken and colleagues treated stroke velocity as a stimulus feature, like orientation

15
00:00:54.203 --> 00:00:59.063
or frequency. They brushed the forearm at a range of controlled velocities.

16
00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:03.023
They recorded single CT afferents in healthy volunteers.

17
00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:09.703
Firing traced an inverted U. It was highest for strokes between about 1 and 10 cm/s.

18
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:12.353
Now a second experiment.

19
00:01:12.993 --> 00:01:17.453
In separate sessions, a separate group of volunteers rated how pleasant the same

20
00:01:17.453 --> 00:01:22.833
strokes felt. Their ratings traced the same inverted U. But the two curves came from

21
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:23.833
different people.

22
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.697
Think of two thermometers in different rooms.

23
00:01:27.197 --> 00:01:28.397
Both read highest at noon.

24
00:01:29.057 --> 00:01:31.437
They agree, but neither shows that one causes the other.

25
00:01:31.877 --> 00:01:32.937
The same is true here.

26
00:01:33.297 --> 00:01:34.537
The result is a correspondence.

27
00:01:35.257 --> 00:01:38.417
It does not show that CT firing is needed for pleasantness.

28
00:01:38.897 --> 00:01:40.417
A later study adds a limit.

29
00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:45.337
It reported 19 patients tested before and after a cut to the anterolateral spinal

30
00:01:45.337 --> 00:01:49.137
cord. Brush pleasantness ratings did not significantly change.

31
00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:51.637
That rules out one obligatory route.

32
00:01:51.637 --> 00:01:54.957
It does not say which route carries the signal instead.

33
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:57.787
There is one more reason for care.

34
00:01:58.487 --> 00:02:02.387
In mouse hairy skin, at least three afferent populations are candidates for

35
00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:03.387
detecting a stroke.

36
00:02:03.687 --> 00:02:04.887
They are not the same cells.

37
00:02:05.307 --> 00:02:08.467
So gentle stroking is not a job for C fibres alone.

38
00:02:09.127 --> 00:02:12.227
Keep the rule. Two matching curves are a correspondence.

39
00:02:12.907 --> 00:02:14.647
They do not show which fibre is needed.
